#%PAM-1.0
# PAM service file for saola-lockscreen (src/auth.rs's SERVICE constant).
# Install as /etc/pam.d/saola-lockscreen — see contrib/session/README.md for
# the exact root command and why this supersedes an older interim command
# from Stage 4's handoff.
#
# A locker calls exactly two PAM functions (src/auth.rs's `run_pam`):
# pam_authenticate() and pam_acct_mgmt(). It never opens a session and never
# changes a password, so this file deliberately has no `session` or
# `password` stack — there would be nothing to run them for.
#
# `account include system-auth` is not optional. Stage 4's original handoff
# printed an `auth include system-auth`-only version of this file; Stage 6's
# security review (docs/REVIEW-v0.1.md, finding H-2) found that with no
# `account` stack at all, pam_acct_mgmt() returns PAM_PERM_DENIED — Linux-PAM
# does NOT fall back to /etc/pam.d/other for a service file that exists but
# has an empty phase, only for a service file that is missing entirely. The
# result: a correct password authenticates, then the account check fails,
# and src/auth.rs's classify_failure() (before Stage 7's M-4 fix) reported
# "Wrong password." for every attempt, correct or not, while still burning
# the pam_faillock budget on the auth phase's own bookkeeping. See H-2's
# full write-up for the failure trace. Reference for a service that
# correctly runs both phases: /usr/lib/pam.d/polkit-1.
auth      include   system-auth
account   include   system-auth

# rosec auto-unlock — the pam_exec fallback form, not the native
# pam_rosec.so module. Per https://jmylchreest.github.io/rosec/pam
# ("Fallback: pam_exec (no native module)"), the pam_exec approach "only
# works for screen unlock (not initial login), because `pam_exec
# expose_authtok` runs during the auth phase only — there is no
# stash-and-replay for the session phase." That is exactly this crate's
# situation (PLAN.md's Architecture decision): a locker never runs PAM's
# session phase, so the native module's session-phase helper
# (rosec-pam-unlock, normally invoked via `session include rosec`) would
# never fire here even if wired in — only the auth-phase pam_exec form can
# reach it. `expose_authtok` hands the just-verified password to the helper
# via PAM's own authtok stash (not argv, not an env var); `quiet` suppresses
# the helper's stdout/stderr going to the session's journal; `optional`
# means this line can never itself block or deny the unlock (defense in
# depth — rosec's own module already returns PAM_SUCCESS on every one of its
# error paths, per that doc page's "Security" section). Placed after the
# `auth include system-auth` line above, per the doc's own example ("add
# after auth").
auth      optional  pam_exec.so  expose_authtok quiet /usr/lib/rosec/rosec-pam-unlock
